data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 10, the Cosmos DB provider deprecated synchronous query execution and made it throw by default, but you could opt back in with:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(...).ConfigureWarnings(w =>
    w.Ignore(CosmosEventId.SyncNotSupported));
```

**In EF Core 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** Sync-over-async wrappers for the Cosmos provider are gone. Calling `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc. on Cosmos `DbSet`s now throws `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally.

## Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` remains synchronous (it only tracks in memory).
- All query execution (`ToList`, `First`, `Count`) and `SaveChanges` must become async.
- Callers up the stack will also need to become `async`/`await` — this typically ripples up to the controller level (which ASP.NET Core already supports natively).
